@page "/guide"

<PageTitle>How it works · Schmidley</PageTitle>

<section class="page-heading compact guide-heading">
    <div>
        <h1>How it works</h1>
        <p class="lede">From the first source scan to a message published on Discord.</p>
    </div>
</section>

<section class="guide-flow" aria-label="Workflow">
    <article><span>01</span><h2>Refresh a source</h2><p>Open <a href="/sources">Sources</a>. Refresh one source from its card, or scan every source at once.</p></article>
    <article><span>02</span><h2>Review the result</h2><p>After a scan, the card shows the number of items read, created, and changed. Its link opens the content from that source.</p></article>
    <article><span>03</span><h2>Open the preview</h2><p>Select a title in the index. The left column shows extracted blocks; the right one shows the exact rich embeds and payload prepared for Discord.</p></article>
    <article><span>04</span><h2>Approve or schedule</h2><p>Content from sources that require review waits for approval. A manual entry can be saved as a draft or scheduled for publication.</p></article>
</section>

<section class="guide-reference">
    <article>
        <h2>Discord message format</h2>
        <p>The embed title links to the source. Text and ordinary links form its description, key/value data becomes fields, and images become the primary image and subsequent embeds. A YouTube URL remains in the message body so Discord can render its own player; this requires the Embed Links permission. The preview and publisher use the same composer output.</p>
    </article>
    <article>
        <h2>Splitting happens automatically</h2>
        <p>The bot observes Discord's 4,096-character description limit, 25 fields per embed, 10 embeds, and 6,000 embed characters per message. Larger entries are split into multiple updatable Discord messages.</p>
    </article>
</section>

<section class="guide-reference">
    <article>
        <h2>First source refresh</h2>
        <p>The first successful scan stores the source state. Later scans compare a document hash against PostgreSQL. The bot creates a revision and publication only after finding new or changed content, according to the source trust policy.</p>
    </article>
    <article>
        <p class="eyebrow">STATUSES</p>
        <dl class="guide-definitions">
            <div><dt>Draft</dt><dd>An entry without a due time, or material waiting for review.</dd></div>
            <div><dt>Scheduled</dt><dd>A post waiting for its scheduled delivery time.</dd></div>
            <div><dt>Published</dt><dd>Discord returned a message identifier.</dd></div>
            <div><dt>Archive</dt><dd>The dashboard shows why it was archived: manual, expired, or superseded. A manually archived item is not reactivated by a refetch.</dd></div>
        </dl>
    </article>
</section>

<section class="guide-reference">
    <article>
        <h2>Add an announcement outside automatic sources</h2>
        <p>Open <a href="/content/new">New entry</a>, choose a game and type, then add text, links, and image URLs. Date and time use the browser's timezone while the database stores them in UTC.</p>
    </article>
    <article>
        <h2>Check the source card and logs</h2>
        <p>A failed scan does not remove previous data. The card keeps the attempt time and a shortened error message. Full details are available in the container logs.</p>
        <pre><code>docker compose logs --tail 200 gachabot</code></pre>
    </article>
</section>
